item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q006_ubiquitin_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 supports probable common ancestry among three distinct families: Ubiquitin-related (d.15.1.1; SUNID 54237), UBX domain (d.15.1.2; 54250), and Ras-binding domain, RBD (d.15.1.5; 54263). All belong to the Ubiquitin-like superfamily (d.15.1; 54236), within the beta-Grasp (ubiquitin-like) fold (d.15; 54235) and Alpha and beta proteins (a+b) class. The shared superfamily supplies the evolutionary inference; fold or class similarity alone would not. Their separate families describe closer relationships within this broader evolutionary grouping.

Biologically, this connects a conserved domain scaffold with divergent functions and makes the families useful candidates for comparative structural analysis and remote-homology recognition. It does not make their functions interchangeable. Ubiquitin can act as a covalent protein modifier, UBX domains commonly mediate interactions with p97/VCP, and RBDs recognize Ras-family GTPases. Those biological examples are background knowledge, not functions established for every member by the retrieved hierarchy.

It would be unsafe to annotate a UBX domain or RBD as a ubiquitin-like conjugatable modifier, infer ubiquitination or proteasomal targeting from superfamily membership, transfer Ras-binding specificity to ubiquitin-related or UBX proteins, or transfer p97/VCP binding to all members. Even the Ubiquitin-related family does not guarantee conjugation activity. Specific annotation requires sequence and terminal-motif evidence, conservation of the relevant interaction surface, domain architecture and cellular context, and biochemical binding or conjugation evidence, ideally supported by partner-bound structures and literature. The shared scaffold can guide template searches, but does not establish interchangeable interfaces or whole-protein functions.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","Pretrained biological knowledge","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=Ras-binding&mode=auto&limit=15","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=UBX&mode=auto&limit=5","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15.1.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54237","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54237/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54237/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54237/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54250","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54250/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54250/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54250/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54263","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54263/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54263/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54263/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54235","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54235/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54235/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54235/homology"]}]}
